    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Count frequencies.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction topKFrequent(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  k: number,\n): number[] {\n  if (k <= 0) return [];\n\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = Object.create(null);\n\n  for (const value of nums) {\n    const key = String(value);\n    counts[key] = (counts[key] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n\n  const entries = Object.keys(counts).map((key) => ({\n    value: Number(key),\n    frequency: counts[key]!,\n  }));\n\n  entries.sort((a, b) => b.frequency - a.frequency);\n\n  return entries.slice(0, k).map((entry) => entry.value);\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — WITH BUILT-IN Map:\n```ts\nfunction topKFrequentUsingMap(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  k: number,\n): number[] {\n  const counts = new Map<number, number>();\n\n  for (const value of nums) {\n    counts.set(value, (counts.get(value) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n\n  return [...counts.entries()]\n    .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])\n    .slice(0, k)\n    .map(([value]) => value);\n}\n```\n\nStep 3 — Example:\n[1,1,1,2,2,3], k=2\nfrequencies: 1→3, 2→2, 3→1\nanswer [1,2].\n\nStep 4 — Optimization:\nFor many distinct values and small k, use a min-heap of size k after counting: O(n + m log k), where m is distinct-value count.",
